        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Does liquid rubber stop hydrostatic pressure?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "High-solids elastomeric membranes resist substantial water pressure and are used below grade on foundation exteriors for that reason. But no interior coating solves water arriving in volume — if the perimeter drain has failed or the water table rises above the slab, the fix is drainage. Coatings handle seepage and dampness, not a drainage failure.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can either product be applied to a damp wall?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Liquid rubber is the more tolerant of the two — several formulations are rated for application to damp, though not wet, masonry, which is a genuine advantage in a basement that never fully dries. DRYLOK needs a dry, clean, bare surface to bond properly, and applying it to damp masonry is a common cause of early failure.",
          },
        },
