    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-16 lg:py-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic border-l-4 border-[#38BDF8] pl-6 bg-gray-50 py-4 rounded-r-xl">
          Ask one question before you buy either: does this wall move? A rigid coating on a stable wall is cheaper, tintable and rated for pressure. A rigid coating on a wall that cracks seasonally splits along the crack and takes your weekend with it.
        </p>

        <h2 id="what-they-are" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          1. What Each Product Actually Is
        </h2>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">DRYLOK</h3>
        <p>
          DRYLOK, made by UGL, is a cementitious latex masonry waterproofer — Portland cement suspended in a latex binder. Brushed into bare masonry, the cement fraction reacts with residual moisture and free lime in the concrete, so the coating becomes chemically integrated with the top layer of the wall rather than sitting on it. That integration is what allows the Extreme formula's 15 psi hydrostatic pressure rating and its ten-year warranty.
        </p>
        <p>
          It is applied in two full coats, needs bare and thoroughly prepared masonry, and dries to a slightly textured, paint-like finish that can be tinted or topcoated. Coverage runs about 75 to 100 square feet per gallon per coat on block.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Liquid Rubber</h3>
        <p>
          "Liquid rubber" covers a family of high-solids elastomeric membranes — Ames Blue Max and Liquid Rubber's foundation sealant are the two most commonly bought for basements. They are applied thickly by roller or brush and cure into a genuinely elastic membrane with substantial elongation: the cured film can stretch materially before it tears.
        </p>
        <p>
          It bonds mechanically rather than chemically, tolerates a damp substrate better than DRYLOK does, and can be used on the exterior face of a foundation below grade as well as inside. The cured surface is rubbery and matte, and it will not take ordinary wall paint.
        </p>

        <!-- Head-to-Head Comparison Table -->
        <div class="my-16 bg-gradient-to-br from-gray-950 to-gray-900 rounded-3xl p-8 lg:p-12 text-white shadow-2xl">
          <h2 id="head-to-head" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-white mt-0 mb-8 border-none text-center">Head-to-Head Comparison</h2>
          <div class="overflow-x-auto">
            <table class="w-full text-sm text-left border-collapse">
              <thead>
                <tr class="border-b border-gray-700">
                  <th class="py-3 px-4 text-[#38BDF8] font-bold text-base">Category</th>
                  <th class="py-3 px-4 text-[#38BDF8] font-bold text-base">Liquid Rubber</th>
                  <th class="py-3 px-4 text-[#FF4500] font-bold text-base">DRYLOK</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
