        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            <strong class="text-white">Liquid rubber and DRYLOK</strong> are the rare pair of waterproofing products that really do compete for the same wall. The difference is rigidity: DRYLOK integrates with the masonry and resists pressure but cannot move, while an elastomeric membrane stretches over cracks but costs more and cannot be painted.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
            Decide on movement, not on price. A stable wall takes DRYLOK, two coats onto bare prepared masonry, and looks like a finished wall afterwards. A wall that cracks takes the membrane, and will still be dry when the rigid coating would have split. And whichever you choose, if water is arriving after every storm, the coating is the last thing to buy — the drainage outside comes first.
          </p>
        </div>

